    "datePublished": "2026-07-27",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-27",
    "description": "Five recurring errors teams make in a SaaS spend audit — missing annual tools, lumping unrelated apps, ignoring seat counts, skipping forgotten trials, and never re-auditing — with fixes.",
    "html": "<h2>Mistake 1 — auditing only the monthly tools</h2>\n<p>The most common error is listing the tools that bill monthly and forgetting the annual ones. An annual $600 tool is $50/month that never shows up in a monthly-only list, so it slips past the duplicate check entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> convert every annual price to a monthly figure before you audit, so all tools sit in the same unit and none hide in a once-a-year charge.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 2 — grouping by vendor instead of by function</h2>\n<p>If you group tools by who sells them, two chat apps from different vendors look unrelated and the overlap is missed. Tool sprawl is a <em>function</em> problem, not a vendor problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> group by what the tool does — chat, docs, meetings, design, project management. SaaSpend does this grouping automatically and produces a <strong>deterministic duplicate-category checklist</strong>, so the same list always returns the same flags.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 3 — ignoring seat counts</h2>\n<p>A tool licensed for 50 seats but used by 20 is a leak even if it is not duplicated. Category flags catch overlaps; they do not catch over-provisioned seats.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> add a seat-count review as a manual step alongside the audit. The duplicate-category and forgotten-tool flags tell you <em>what</em> overlaps; seat counts tell you <em>how much</em> you are over-paying on tools you keep.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 4 — skipping the forgotten trials</h2>\n<p>Trials that became permanent charges are the quiet leaks. They are small individually and easy to miss in a long list, but they add up and they rarely have an owner.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> let <strong>forgotten-tool flags</strong> surface tools with no clear owner or no recent activity. Then assign each one a yes/no decision instead of letting it renew silently.</p>\n<h2>Mistake 5 — never re-auditing</h2>\n<p>An audit is a snapshot. Run it once, act on it, and then let the list go stale — the leaks return. Forgotten trials become permanent, new duplicates accumulate after hires, and the next surprise invoice arrives.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> re-run the audit each quarter and after every renewal wave. Because SaaSpend's flags are deterministic, you can compare this quarter's output to last quarter's and see exactly what changed.</p>\n<h2>The honest limit</h2>\n<p>No audit — manual or tool-assisted — <em>guarantees</em> a dollar saving. The <strong>suggested savings estimate</strong> is exactly that: an estimate from the list you enter, assuming you consolidate duplicate categories. Real savings land only when someone actually cancels or migrates, and your real contract terms may change the math.</p>\n",
