    "html": "<h2>The questions that come up first</h2>\n<p>Most teams ask the same handful of questions the first time they face tool sprawl. This roundup answers them plainly, with the honest limits stated up front.</p>\n<h2>What is a SaaS spend audit, really?</h2>\n<p>It is a structured review of every subscription your team pays for, what it costs, and whether you still need it. The output is not just a list — it is a set of flags: duplicate categories, forgotten tools, and a suggested savings estimate. The list tells you what you bought; the flags tell you what to question.</p>\n<h2>How does SaaSpend estimate savings?</h2>\n<p>You paste your tools (one per line with monthly cost) and your team size. SaaSpend groups them by function, flags duplicate categories, marks likely-forgotten tools, and calculates a <strong>suggested savings figure</strong> from consolidating each duplicate category to one tool.</p>\n<p>The number is an <em>estimate from the list you enter</em> — not a guaranteed saving. It assumes you actually consolidate, and it cannot see your real contract terms in the Free tier.</p>\n<h2>Does it connect to my billing?</h2>\n<p>In the Free tier, no. You supply the list, and the audit reflects exactly what you provide. Pro adds alerts on new subscriptions and export; Enterprise supports BYOK for higher caps. If continuous billing connection matters to you, that is the direction the paid tiers move.</p>\n<h2>How often should we run it?</h2>\n<p>At least quarterly, and again after any major hire, department change, or renewal wave. Because the flags are deterministic, re-running lets you compare this quarter's output to last quarter's and see exactly what changed.</p>\n<h2>What does the Free tier cover?</h2>\n<p>One spend audit run per day and a watermarked export. That is enough to learn the format and audit a small stack. Paid Pro ($19/mo) adds 300 runs per month, alerts, and a clean export; Enterprise is custom with BYOK and multi-department rulesets.</p>\n<h2>The one thing to remember</h2>\n<p>No audit — manual or tool-assisted — <em>guarantees</em> a dollar saving. The value is in making overlap and forgotten tools visible and repeatable, so the decisions stop hiding in a stale spreadsheet.</p>\n",
