    "description": "A SaaS spend audit is a structured review of every subscription your team pays for — flagging duplicate categories, forgotten tools, and an estimate of what you could consolidate. Here is how it works and where SaaSpend fits.",
    "html": "<h2>What a SaaS spend audit actually is</h2>\n<p>A <strong>SaaS spend audit</strong> is a structured review of every software subscription your team pays for, what it costs, and whether you still need it. The goal is not accounting for its own sake — it is to surface the money leaking through duplicate tools, forgotten trials, and seats nobody uses.</p>\n<p>Most teams start an audit with a single question: <em>\"How much are we actually paying for software?\"</em> The answer is usually higher than anyone expects, because SaaS purchases happen in many places — a corporate card here, a department budget there — and the list is rarely kept in one place.</p>\n<h2>Why a spreadsheet stops scaling</h2>\n<p>A spreadsheet works for your first ten tools. It breaks down around thirty, when three problems appear at once:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Categories blur.</strong> Two chat apps, two doc wikis, and three video tools all sit in one long list with no signal about which ones overlap.</li><li><strong>Costs go stale.</strong> Annual tools, seat-based pricing, and mid-year renewals drift out of date the moment you stop updating the sheet.</li><li><strong>Nobody owns it.</strong> When the person who built the sheet leaves, the audit goes dark until the next surprise invoice.</li></ul>\n<p>An audit's value is in the <em>flagging</em>, not the listing. A list tells you what you bought; a flagged audit tells you what to question.</p>\n<h2>What a good audit flags</h2>\n<p>A useful SaaS spend audit produces three outputs, not one:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Duplicate-category flags</strong> — groups tools by what they do (chat, docs, meetings, design) and marks the categories where you run more than one.</li><li><strong>Forgotten-tool flags</strong> — surfaces tools with no clear owner, no recent activity, or a renewal nobody remembers approving.</li><li><strong>A suggested savings number</strong> — an <em>estimate</em> of what consolidating the duplicate categories could remove from your monthly bill.</li></ul>\n<p>SaaSpend takes the list you paste — one tool per line with its monthly USD cost — and returns exactly these three outputs. The duplicate detection is deterministic: the same input list always produces the same flags, so you can re-run it after every renewal and compare results.</p>\n<h2>How SaaSpend runs the audit</h2>\n<p>You list your SaaS tools (for example <code>Slack, 8</code> and <code>Discord, 7</code> on separate lines) and your team size, then run the audit. SaaSpend groups the tools by category, flags the duplicate categories, marks likely-forgotten tools, and shows a suggested savings figure.</p>\n<p>The savings number is a <strong>suggested estimate</strong>, not a promise. It assumes you consolidate each duplicate category down to a single tool — which only happens if you actually cancel or migrate. The audit shows the opportunity; the decision and the action stay with you.</p>\n<h2>What a SaaS spend audit cannot do</h2>\n<p>An audit reduces obvious waste and makes overlap visible. It is <strong>not</strong> a guarantee that you will save a specific dollar amount, and it is not a replacement for reviewing each tool with the team that uses it. In the Free tier, SaaSpend does not connect to your billing accounts — you supply the list, and the estimate reflects only what you enter.</p>\n<p>Treat the audit as a flashlight, not a refund.</p>\n",
